Closest Sum Pair Across Two Arrays

Given two sorted arrays arr1[] and arr2[] of size n and m and a number x, find the pair whose sum is closest to x and the pair has an element 
from each array. In the case of multiple closest pairs return any one of them.

Explanation:
Closest Pair — Two Pointer Idea

Sorted arrays → use two pointers from opposite ends.

left = 0 → smallest element of arr1
right = arr2.length - 1 → largest element of arr2
This gives us control in both directions:
  sum < x → need a bigger sum → left++
  sum > x → need a smaller sum → right--
At every step, check |sum - x| and keep the minimum.

Why opposite ends?
If both pointers started from the left, both movements would only increase the sum. 
Starting from opposite ends lets one pointer increase the sum and the other decrease it.

Think: “I need to move the sum toward x — which pointer can help me do that?”


Code:
class Solution {
    public static ArrayList<Integer> findClosestPair(int arr1[], int arr2[], int x) {
        // code here
        int left=0;
        int right= arr2.length-1;
        int bestA=0;
        int bestB = 0;
        int minDiff = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        ArrayList<Integer> ans =new ArrayList<>();
        
        while(left<arr1.length && right>=0){
            int sum = arr1[left]+arr2[right];
            int diff = Math.abs(x-sum);
            
            if(diff<minDiff){
                minDiff = diff;
                bestA = arr1[left];
                bestB = arr2[right];
            }
            
            if(sum<x){
                left++;
            }
            else{
                right--;
            }
            
        }
        
        ans.add(bestA); ans.add(bestB);
        return ans;
    }
}
